<BaseLayout title="Research">

  <section class="research-intro shell">
    <p class="kicker">Research</p>
    <p>
      <strong>Research Vision.</strong> PAWSSEC (Provable Assurance for Whole-System
      Security) is a long-term research vision for achieving end-to-end,
      machine-checked security guarantees across the computing stack, from
      programming languages and compilers to systems and hardware.
    </p>

      <p> My research lies at the intersection of programming languages, formal
      verification, compiler technology, and systems security. I develop
      mathematical foundations and practical tools for building software whose
      correctness and security properties can be established rigorously and
      preserved across different layers of the software stack. My work spans
      language design, verified compilation, decompilation, systems software, and emerging
      applications of formal methods to AI systems.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="research-area shell">
    <h2>Programming Languages</h2>

    <p>
      Programming languages provide the foundation for building reliable and secure software. 
      My research develops verification-friendly
      programming languages, formal semantics, 
      and type systems that make correctness and security properties explicit and 
      amenable to machine-checked verification.
    </p>

    <div class="tags" aria-label="Programming languages keywords">
      <span><strong>Keywords:</strong></span>
      <span>Language design</span>
      <span>Formal semantics</span>
      <span>Type systems</span>
      <span>Static analysis</span>
      <span>Domain-specific languages</span>
      <span>Rocq</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Representative Projects</h3>

    <ul class="project-list">
  <li>
    <strong>BeePL.</strong> A verification-friendly language and verified
    compilation framework for Linux eBPF.

    <div class="project-publications">
      <strong>Publications:</strong>
      <a href="https://youtu.be/lczetpAgPo0?si=RnnT6_GS30vzYfDE">PriSC 2025</a>,
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.09883">arXiv</a>
    </div>

    <div class="project-links">
      <a href="https://github.com/pawssec/beepl_compcert">GitHub</a>
    </div>
  </li>

   <li>
    <strong>Jasmin.</strong> A verification-oriented programming language for high-performance cryptographic software. 
    My research extends its formal foundations through structured leakage models, fine-grained constant-time policies, 
    and verified compilation that preserves security guarantees.

    <div class="project-links">
      <a href="https://github.com/jasmin-lang/jasmin">GitHub</a> ·
      <a href="#formal-methods-security">Related security research and publications</a>
    </div>
  </li>

  <li>
    <strong>λir.</strong> A language with intensional receive.
    <div class="project-publications">
      <strong>Publications:</strong>
      <a href="https://www.proquest.com/openview/950db6495c95bfabb23e11b90fc49a04/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&cbl=18750">Master's thesis</a>
    </div>

    <div class="project-links">
      <a href="https://github.com/swarnpriya/intensional-receive">GitHub</a>
    </div>
  </li>
</ul>
  </section>

  <section class="research-area shell">
    <h2>Verified Compilation/Decompilation and Toolchains</h2>

    <p>
      Modern software relies on complex compilation toolchains where correctness and security guarantees established at 
      the source level can be compromised by compiler transformations or supporting infrastructure. 
      My research develops verified compilation techniques and formally verified toolchains that preserve 
      these guarantees throughout the software build process, from compilation to linking, loading, and code generation.
    </p>

    <div class="tags" aria-label="Compilation and toolchains keywords">
      <span><strong>Keywords:</strong></span>
      <span>Verified compilation</span>
      <span>Decompilation</span>
      <span>Translation validation</span>
      <span>Semantic preservation</span>
      <span>CompCert</span>
      <span>Linking and loading</span>
      <span>Rocq</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Representative Projects</h3>

  <ul class="project-list">
    <li>
      <strong>BeePL.</strong>
      A verification-friendly language for Linux eBPF with a formally verified
      compilation path through CompCert.
      <div class="project-publications">
        <strong>Publications:</strong>
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/search/?query=BeePL%3A+Correct-by-compilation+kernel+extensions&amp;searchtype=all">arXiv</a>
      </div>
      <div class="project-links">
        <span>Ongoing work</span>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li id="decompcert">
      <strong>DeCompCert.</strong>
      A decompilation framework that reconstructs C from CompCert-generated AArch64 assembly through
      CompCert’s intermediate languages. It combines inference-guided reconstruction
      with formally verified transformations and translation validation, establishing
      machine-checked semantic guarantees between accepted C reconstructions and
      their typed assembly inputs.
      <div class="project-publications">
        <strong>Publications:</strong>{' '}
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18055">arXiv</a>
      </div>
      <div class="project-links">
        <strong>Website:</strong>{' '}
        <a class="project-website-link" href="https://pawssec.github.io/decompcert_website/#team">
          <img src="/images/decompcert-logo.svg" alt="" width="40" height="40" />
          DeCompCert
        </a>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li>
      <strong>Verified Linking and Loading.</strong>
      Developing machine-checked linkers and loaders that preserve the
      semantics and security properties established during compilation,
      extending formal assurance from compiled components to executable
      programs.

      <div class="project-links">
        <span>Under submission</span>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ul>

    <p class="project-links">
      Related work on security-preserving compilation:{' '}
      <a href="#structured-leakage">Structured Leakage</a>,{' '}
      <a href="#fine-grained-constant-time">Fine-Grained Constant-Time Security</a>,{' '}
      <a href="#spectre-defenses">Spectre defenses</a>, and{' '}
      <a href="#vellsslh">VellSSLH</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="research-area shell" id="formal-methods-security">
    <h2>Formal Methods for Security</h2>

    <p>
      My research applies formal methods to establish rigorous security guarantees
      for software systems. I develop formal models, type systems, and
      machine-checked proofs that connect security policies with practical
      verification and compiler-based defenses. In particular, my work addresses
      timing side channels and speculative-execution attacks, with applications
      to high-performance cryptographic software.
    </p>

    <div class="tags" aria-label="Security keywords">
      <span><strong>Keywords:</strong></span>
      <span>Software security</span>
      <span>Information flow</span>
      <span>Constant-time security</span>
      <span>Speculative Constant-time Security</span>
      <span>High-assurance cryptography</span>
      <span>LLVM IR</span>
      <span>Vellvm</span>
      <span>Rocq</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Representative Projects</h3>

    <ul class="project-list">
    <li id="structured-leakage">
      <strong>Structured Leakage.</strong>
      A formal framework for describing program leakage in a structured way and
      relating leakage across source and target languages. It provides the semantic
      foundation for reasoning about cryptographic constant-time security and its
      preservation through compilation.

      <div class="project-publications">
      <strong>Publications:</strong>
      <a href="https://inria.hal.science/hal-03430789">CCS 2021</a>
      </div>

      <div class="project-links">
      <a href="https://github.com/jasmin-lang/jasmin/tree/constant-time">GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li id="fine-grained-constant-time">
      <strong>Fine-Grained Constant-Time Security.</strong>
      Extending structured leakage with fine-grained leakage models that account
      for cache-line observations and variable-time instructions. The work provides
      source-level verification and a formally verified compilation infrastructure
      that preserves constant-time security for the selected leakage model.

      <div class="project-publications">
      <strong>Publications:</strong>
      <a href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2022/630.pdf">CCS 2022</a>
      </div>

      <div class="project-links">
      <a href="https://github.com/jasmin-lang/jasmin/tree/constant-time-op">GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li id="spectre-defenses">
      <strong>Spectre Defenses for Cryptographic Software.</strong>
      Formal methods for protecting high-performance cryptographic software
      against speculative-execution attacks, including type-based defenses
      against Spectre v1 in Jasmin.

      <div class="project-publications">
        <strong>Publications:</strong>{' '}
        <a href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2022/1270.pdf">S&amp;P 2023</a>{' '}
        <span class="award">(Distinguished Paper Award)</span>,{' '}
        <a href="https://inria.hal.science/hal-03352062">S&amp;P 2021</a>
      </div>

      <div class="project-links">
        <a href="https://github.com/jasmin-lang/jasmin">GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li id="vellsslh">
      <strong>VellSSLH: Selective Speculative Load Hardening.</strong>
      A type-guided approach to selectively hardening C programs against Spectre v1
      at LLVM IR. The work extends Vellvm with speculative execution and leakage
      observations, and uses VellSCT, a security checker proved sound in Rocq, to
      validate speculative constant-time security under stated runtime assumptions.
      A separate proof establishes that a reference hardening transformation
      preserves architectural behavior.
      <div class="project-publications">
        <strong>Publications:</strong>{' '}
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.18463">arXiv</a>
      </div>
    </li>

  </ul>
  </section>

    <section class="research-area shell">
    <h2>Formal Methods for AI Systems</h2>

    <p>
      Large language models and autonomous agents introduce new challenges for
      correctness, security, and reliability. I am exploring how ideas from
      programming languages, compiler technology, and formal verification can
      provide principled foundations for AI systems through structured prompt
      representations, policy enforcement, and verification techniques for
      agentic workflows.
    </p>

    <div class="tags" aria-label="AI systems keywords">
      <span><strong>Keywords:</strong></span>
      <span>LLM agents</span>
      <span>Authorization</span>
      <span>Policy verification</span>
      <span>Prompt compilation</span>
      <span>Workflow preservation</span>
      <span>Lean</span>
      <span>Rocq</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Representative Projects</h3>

  <ul class="project-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Verified Prompt Compilation.</strong>
      Compiler-inspired frameworks for systematically improving the correctness, safety, and reliability of 
      natural language prompts through formal intermediate representations and verified transformations.
      <div class="project-links">
        <span>Ongoing work</span>
      </div>
    </li>

    

    <li>
      <strong>Agenra.</strong>
      A policy language and Lean-verified enforcement core for stateful LLM agent
      authorization. Agenra tracks runtime resources and consumable permissions,
      and uses simulation proofs to certify that policy revisions preserve
      permitted workflows under stated assumptions. The framework treats
      workflow preservation and safety as distinct properties.
      <div class="project-publications">
        <strong>Publications:</strong>{' '}
        <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.22868">arXiv</a>
      </div>
    </li>
    </ul>
</section>

</BaseLayout>
